# Copy to .env and adjust.
#
# `make run` (a single container) connects to the database named by all of these.
# From inside the container, the host machine is reachable as
# host.docker.internal (Docker Desktop).
#
# `docker compose up` (`make up`) runs its own database and sets POSTGRES_HOST and
# POSTGRES_PORT itself. It uses only POSTGRES_DB, POSTGRES_USER and
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD from here, to create the database; changing the password
# later does not change it in an existing volume. BACKEND_PORT (default 8000) is
# the port the backend is published on, and FRONTEND_PORT (default 5173) the port
# of the frontend.
POSTGRES_HOST=host.docker.internal
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_DB=tiron
POSTGRES_USER=tiron
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
# BACKEND_PORT=8000
# FRONTEND_PORT=5173
# The RabbitMQ broker of `docker compose up`, used by the Celery worker. It is not
# published on the host, so the defaults (tiron / tiron) are fine for development.
# RABBITMQ_USER=tiron
# RABBITMQ_PASSWORD=tiron

# For a database that is not in Docker (Cloud SQL, say). Optional: TLS for the
# connection, `require`, `verify-ca` or `verify-full` (without it, libpq's default
# `prefer` is used), and the CA certificate that `verify-ca` and `verify-full` check
# the server against. Not needed with the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy (it encrypts) or with
# `docker compose up`.
# POSTGRES_SSLMODE=verify-ca
# POSTGRES_SSLROOTCERT=/certs/server-ca.pem
